AuroraConfig::blockingPipelines: wait for a draw's pipeline instead of skipping the draw

GameCube behaviour: the GPU draws every primitive the game sends, every frame; there is no
shader compilation and nothing is ever left out of a frame. Dolphin's default shader
compilation mode (VideoCommon ShaderCompilationMode::Synchronous) keeps this: a draw waits
for its shader. Its "asynchronous (skip drawing)" mode is an option.

Aurora 3227d76 resolves every GX and clear pipeline with PipelinePriority::Normal
(lib/gfx/pipeline_cache.cpp find_pipeline): a pipeline that is not built yet goes on the
compile thread's queue and the draw is skipped until it is ready. That is a fine default
for playing, but it makes frames depend on wall-clock compile time. In the game's port,
with several game processes compiling at once (the boot sweep, parallel regression runs),
about 300 pipelines were still queued 1100 frames into Ojhous and Orca's message box drew
its panel, Next button and arrow but not the text or most of the HUD (render audit A3),
in 2 to 7 runs out of 10, while the game state was identical.

The patch adds a config field, false by default (Aurora's behaviour is unchanged). When
it is true, GX and clear pipelines use the existing PipelinePriority::Blocking path: the
pipeline goes to the front of the compile queue (or is promoted from the warm-up queue)
and find_pipeline waits for it. The game's harness turns it on for runs that capture
frames (COS_SHOT), so screenshots show what the console would draw.

Upstream status: not reported upstream yet (encounter/aurora at 3227d76).

diff -ru a/include/aurora/aurora.h b/include/aurora/aurora.h
--- a/include/aurora/aurora.h	2026-10-03 21:48:06
+++ b/include/aurora/aurora.h	2026-10-03 21:48:14
@@ -116,6 +116,13 @@
    * This can be set to 0 to disable allocating this region.
    */
   uint32_t mem2Size;
+
+  /*
+   * Compile GX and clear pipelines synchronously: a draw whose pipeline is not built yet waits for
+   * it instead of being skipped for that frame (the default asynchronous mode). Every frame then
+   * shows every draw, as on the console, at the cost of a stall the first time a pipeline is used.
+   */
+  bool blockingPipelines;
 } AuroraConfig;
 
 typedef struct {
diff -ru a/lib/gfx/pipeline_cache.cpp b/lib/gfx/pipeline_cache.cpp
--- a/lib/gfx/pipeline_cache.cpp	2026-10-03 21:48:06
+++ b/lib/gfx/pipeline_cache.cpp	2026-10-03 21:48:14
@@ -1087,14 +1087,19 @@
   g_pipelineCacheWriteQueue.clear();
 }
 
+// AuroraConfig::blockingPipelines: wait for a draw's pipeline instead of skipping the draw.
+static PipelinePriority draw_pipeline_priority() {
+  return g_config.blockingPipelines ? PipelinePriority::Blocking : PipelinePriority::Normal;
+}
+
 PipelineRef find_pipeline(const clear::PipelineConfig& config, const RenderTargetLayout& layout) {
   remember_pipeline_config(ShaderType::Clear, config, current_frame(), true);
-  return resolve_pipeline(ShaderType::Clear, config, layout, PipelinePriority::Normal);
+  return resolve_pipeline(ShaderType::Clear, config, layout, draw_pipeline_priority());
 }
 
 PipelineRef find_pipeline(const gx::PipelineConfig& config, const RenderTargetLayout& layout) {
   remember_pipeline_config(ShaderType::GX, config, current_frame(), true);
-  return resolve_pipeline(ShaderType::GX, config, layout, PipelinePriority::Normal);
+  return resolve_pipeline(ShaderType::GX, config, layout, draw_pipeline_priority());
 }
 
